## Overview

Extreme droughts are becoming more frequent. During drought, water deficit suppresses plant growth and shifts community composition; it also alters soil microbial activity and the exchange of resources between plants and mycorrhizal fungi, with knock-on effects on nutrient cycling and carbon storage. After rain returns, these effects often persist as *drought legacies* that slow ecosystem recovery.

Do grasslands differ in their resistance to drought and their capacity to recover? How do plants above ground and microbes below ground respond together? Answering these questions requires comparing many grasslands under one common design. The platform was launched in 2018 with a standardised drought experiment at seven sites spanning China's climate zones and grassland types. Our group joined in 2025, focusing on roots, arbuscular mycorrhizal (AM) fungal hyphae and soil microbes to understand how water and nutrient limitation shape belowground symbioses and the ecosystem carbon cycle.

<figure><a href="/images/projects/drought-network/map.jpg"><img src="/images/projects/drought-network/map.jpg" alt="Figure 1. Locations of the drought network sites" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Figure 1. Locations of the drought network sites</figcaption></figure>

## Experimental design

- Randomised block design at each site with two treatments — ambient rainfall (control) and drought (–50% rainfall) — six replicates each, 6 m × 6 m plots.
- Drought intensity was set from each site's rainfall record to match an extreme dry year (below the 10th percentile) and was imposed for eight consecutive years (2018–2025).
- Rainfall is intercepted by clear, UV-transparent rain-out shelters, and ~1 m deep belowground barriers limit lateral water movement.
- In 2026 the shelters were removed to start a 2–3-year recovery phase, comparing how different grasslands recover after long-term drought.

