## What we measure

Every growing season the network surveys plant communities (species composition, height, density, biomass and litter) and measures plant carbon, nitrogen and phosphorus, together with soil physicochemical properties and soil C, N and P.

Our group adds **in-growth cores and hyphal mesh bags** that separate the growth and resource uptake of roots from those of AM fungal hyphae. Combined with hyphal purification, lipidomics and rhizosphere/hyphosphere microbiome analyses, this links plant carbon allocation, the biochemical responses of AM fungi, belowground microbial interactions and soil carbon sequestration.

<figure><a href="/images/projects/drought-network/field.jpg"><img src="/images/projects/drought-network/field.jpg" alt="Figure 2. The field platform and students at work" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Figure 2. The field platform and students at work</figcaption></figure>

## Research questions

1. How do roots and AM fungal hyphae respond to drought and to recovery, and how do they trade off in acquiring and allocating resources?
2. How do drought and recovery reshape rhizosphere and hyphosphere microbial communities and the way they are recruited?
3. Through changes in root–hyphal resource allocation, how do drought and recovery affect the sources, fractions and persistence of soil organic carbon?
